# Caddy fragment for the deployment API.
#
# Where it goes: one copy inside each of the two site blocks, as shown at the
# bottom of this file. Not at the top level, and not in a snippet that is
# imported anywhere else — the whole point of a fixed path list is that it is
# fixed, and a global route would attach the deployment API to every future
# site block by accident.
#
# What Caddy is doing here, and what it is deliberately not doing:
#
#   * It holds no credential. There is no basic_auth, no forward_auth and no
#     header_up carrying a token. The Bearer header travels from the browser
#     straight through to cairn-updater, which validates it itself. Caddy's
#     config file is therefore not a place a deployment token can leak from.
#
#   * `handle` is used rather than `handle_path`. handle_path would strip the
#     matched prefix and the helper would see GET / instead of
#     GET /api/deploy/system/version — every route would 404. The helper
#     matches the full path on purpose, because these four strings are the
#     contract between the two config files.
#
#   * The webtag application never sees these requests. They do not reach
#     127.0.0.1:8732, so the application has no deploy route, no deploy client
#     and nothing to authenticate. Caddy reaches the socket because it runs as
#     the caddy group and the socket is 0660 root:caddy; the webtag account is
#     in neither, so it cannot connect even from inside the same host.
#
#   * Both site blocks get the fragment. reader.alpenl.com is the important
#     one: it is a static tree served from disk, so it keeps answering while
#     webtag.service is stopped, which is exactly the window in which an
#     operator needs to read job status.
#
# The wildcard is the only wildcard: `/api/deploy/system/*` matches the four
# routes and the {id} path segment, and nothing else is proxied to the socket.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paste this block, verbatim, inside BOTH site blocks.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

	handle /api/deploy/system/* {
		reverse_proxy unix//run/cairn-updater.sock {
			# The helper writes Cache-Control: no-store on every response. This
			# just makes sure nothing in front of Caddy re-adds a policy.
			header_down -Cache-Control
			header_down +Cache-Control "no-store"
		}
	}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two site blocks in full, for reference.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# reader.alpenl.com {
# 	handle /api/deploy/system/* {
# 		reverse_proxy unix//run/cairn-updater.sock {
# 			header_down -Cache-Control
# 			header_down +Cache-Control "no-store"
# 		}
# 	}
#
# 	# The root-path Reader, served straight from the root-owned tree the
# 	# helper relinks. 0755 root:root all the way down, so Caddy can read and
# 	# traverse it and the application cannot write to it.
# 	handle {
# 		root * /var/www/reader/current
# 		try_files {path} /index.html
# 		file_server
# 		encode zstd gzip
# 	}
# }
#
# webtag.alpenl.com {
# 	handle /api/deploy/system/* {
# 		reverse_proxy unix//run/cairn-updater.sock {
# 			header_down -Cache-Control
# 			header_down +Cache-Control "no-store"
# 		}
# 	}
#
# 	# Everything else is unchanged: API, /health, /ready and the embedded
# 	# /reader/ build all come from the systemd Core on loopback.
# 	handle {
# 		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8732
# 	}
# }
#
# Caddy sorts `handle` blocks by matcher specificity rather than by file order,
# so the deployment block wins over the catch-all regardless of where it sits.
# It is written first anyway, because a reader should not have to know that.
